# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: dwg-write-rejects-a-drawing-built-in-memory — libredwg refuses the DXF we hand it (error 2048), while a DWG read from disk round-trips
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: drawing-codec
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

Found immediately behind
clautolisp-distributed-native-libraries-not-loaded, once the standalone
program carried the DWG codec (2.2.99). The SHIPPED binary, ActiveX
SaveAs to a .dwg, on a document created in the session:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp-sbcl -norc -q -l x.lsp     ;; (vla-saveas d "…/probe.dwg")
clautolisp: runtime error: COM-SAVE-FAILED: SaveAs could not write
  …/probe.dwg: libredwg failed to write "…/probe.dwg" (error code 2048).
#+end_example

That is progress -- before, the same call said "no writer codec
registered for format :DWG" -- but the write still fails. It fails with
an empty document and with one that has an entity (a LINE added through
vla-AddLine).

A drawing READ FROM A DWG, however, writes back as DWG perfectly:

#+begin_example
(read-drawing "autolisp-front-end/source/empty.dwg")   -> :DWG
(write-drawing d "rt.dxf" :format :dxf-ascii)          -> 37268 bytes
(write-drawing d "rt.dwg" :format :dwg)                -> 302706 bytes
#+end_example

So the shim, the vendored libredwg and the DXF writer all work. What
libredwg refuses is the DXF produced from a drawing this process BUILT,
as opposed to one it parsed.

* Where it is

=dwg-write-drawing= (clautolisp/drawing-dwg/source/codec.lisp) writes a
temporary DXF with =dxf-write-drawing= and calls the shim's
=clal_dxf_to_dwg=, which returns 2048. Codes at or above
=+dwg-err-critical+= (128) are failures, and 2048 is 1<<11 -- one of
libredwg's high error bits, so the first job is to name it from
libredwg's own header rather than report the bare number.

The difference between the two DXFs is the question: a parsed drawing
carries whatever HEADER variables, TABLES entries and handles the source
DWG had, while a drawing built by cador carries only what our own
in-memory model sets. Something libredwg's DXF reader requires is
missing (a candidate list, cheapest first: $ACADVER / $HANDSEED, the
mandatory table records -- VPORT "*ACTIVE*", LTYPE "BYLAYER"/"BYBLOCK"/
"CONTINUOUS", STYLE "STANDARD", APPID "ACAD" -- the BLOCK_RECORD table,
or handle continuity).

* How to find it in one pass

Diff the two DXFs: write the parsed drawing's DXF (works) and the
in-memory drawing's DXF (refused) and compare their HEADER and TABLES
sections. Then bisect by adding to the in-memory drawing what the parsed
one has until libredwg accepts it. Both files are produced by the
snippets above, so this needs no CAD and no Windows.

* Acceptance criteria

- A drawing created in a clautolisp session writes as DWG and reads back,
  with its entities.
- The error message names libredwg's code symbolically, not just the
  number, and says which of the two files it was reading.
- A regression test writes a DWG from an in-memory drawing (not only
  from a parsed one, which is what dwg-write-then-read-round-trips
  covers today) and reads it back.
- The SCHME+ A1 scenario "creation et fermeture d'un DWG temporaire"
  passes.

* Related

- =issues/open/clautolisp-distributed-native-libraries-not-loaded.issue=
  -- the packaging half, which had to be fixed before this became
  visible at all.
- =issues/open/drawing-codec-version-output.issue= -- the writer still
  emits a fixed DWG version (a STUB in dwg-write-drawing); if 2048 turns
  out to be a version rejection, these two meet.
